.TH SET 3 "2026" "y-- standard library" "y-- Value Types"
.SH NAME
set \- built-in set value type methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets in y-- are unordered collections of unique elements. They are highly optimized
for membership testing and mathematical set operations.
.SH "CORE METHODS"
.TP
\fBadd(val)\fR -> set()
Adds \fBval\fR to the set. If it already exists, the set remains unchanged.
.TP
\fBremove(val)\fR -> set()
Removes \fBval\fR from the set. Throws KeyError if \fBval\fR is not in the set.
.TP
\fBdiscard(val)\fR -> set()
Removes \fBval\fR from the set if it is present. Does not throw an error if missing.
.TP
\fBpop()\fR -> any
Removes and returns an arbitrary element from the set. Throws if empty.
.TP
\fBclear()\fR -> set()
Removes all elements from the set.
.TP
\fBreserve(size)\fR -> none()
Pre-allocates bucket capacity to avoid reallocations when growing the set.
.SH "SET OPERATIONS"
.TP
\fBunion(other_set)\fR -> set()
Returns a new set containing all unique elements from both sets.
.TP
\fBintersection(other_set)\fR -> set()
Returns a new set containing only the elements that exist in both sets.
.TP
\fBdifference(other_set)\fR -> set()
Returns a new set with elements that are in this set but not in \fBother_set\fR.
.TP
\fBsymmetric_difference(other_set)\fR -> set()
Returns a new set containing elements that are in one set or the other, but not both.
.TP
\fBissubset(other_set)\fR -> bool()
Returns true if every element in this set is also in \fBother_set\fR.
.TP
\fBissuperset(other_set)\fR -> bool()
Returns true if every element in \fBother_set\fR is also in this set.
.TP
\fBisdisjoint(other_set)\fR -> bool()
Returns true if the two sets share no elements.
.SH "UTILITIES"
.TP
\fBjoin([sep=""])\fR -> string()
Concatenates string representations of the elements. (Order is arbitrary).
.SH "FUNCTIONAL METHODS"
.TP
\fBAll_Of / Any_Of / None_Of / One_Of (lambda)\fR -> bool()
.TP
\fBselect / reject (lambda)\fR -> set()
Filters the set, keeping only elements that match (or don't match) the lambda.
.TP
\fBmap(lambda)\fR -> set()
Maps each element and returns a new set of the results.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
list(3), dict(3)
